  "response": "When liability turns on whether the defendant's own conduct or an independent intervening event produced the harm, a court begins with the allocation of proof, since the party who must persuade the finder of fact on causation ordinarily determines how the dispute ends. A plaintiff must prove causation beyond a reasonable doubt in a negligence action. That requirement cannot be squared with the rule that the plaintiff bears the burden of proving causation by a preponderance of the evidence; no single claim can be governed by both measures of persuasion at once. Foreseeability is the central criterion for deciding whether an intervening act is superseding, and the dominance of that criterion has produced the settled rule that a foreseeable intervening cause does not break the chain of proximate causation. Causation, in the ordinary run of cases, is a question of fact reserved for the jury.\n\nAny intervening act by a third party automatically relieves the original defendant of liability; that proposition cannot be reconciled with the principle that a foreseeable intervening cause leaves the chain of proximate causation intact, and a court cannot apply the two together. Courts accordingly disentangle the competing strands by asking whether the supervening event fell within the range of hazards that the defendant's carelessness created, and by scrutinising the quality of the proof offered on each side of that question. Because expert testimony is commonly used to establish medical causation in personal injury cases, the admissibility of expert causation testimony in federal court came to be governed by the Daubert standard.\n\nContemporaneous maintenance records document the condition of the premises before the injury occurred; the timestamped surveillance footage picks up subsequently, fixing the sequence of events immediately preceding the injury. That footage indicates that the intervening storm contributed to the plaintiff's injury, and the maintenance records show that the defendant's neglected maintenance contributed to the injury as well. At least one of those two forces, the storm or the neglected upkeep, and perhaps both of them together, brought the plaintiff's harm about.\n\nOn such a record the finder of fact must choose between two mutually exclusive dispositions: either the verdict assigned the defendant a share of the fault for the injury, or the verdict assigned the defendant no share of the fault for the injury, and exactly one of those results obtains. What proves decisive, in the end, is the documentary and recorded material that fixes both the physical condition of the place and the timing of what happened there, since records of upkeep and time-stamped imagery, tested for reliability under the governing standards of admissibility, leave the tribunal with a concrete chronology in place of competing hypotheses.",
